1
00:00:00,176 --> 00:00:03,238
Bun has built-in support for various types

2
00:00:03,238 --> 00:00:05,113
of databases you may wanna use in your

3
00:00:05,241 --> 00:00:05,931
applications.

4
00:00:07,005 --> 00:00:09,537
Many applications also need to store files

5
00:00:09,650 --> 00:00:12,920
though, and that's why Bun has a built-in S3

6
00:00:12,920 --> 00:00:15,004
client so that you can use

7
00:00:15,581 --> 00:00:20,518
AWS S3 or any S3-compatible storage provider

8
00:00:20,694 --> 00:00:23,163
like Cloudflare R2 or many others

9
00:00:23,900 --> 00:00:26,145
right from inside Bun, again, without

10
00:00:26,145 --> 00:00:28,405
installing any extra dependencies or

11
00:00:28,405 --> 00:00:30,521
packages. It's all built in, it supports

12
00:00:30,633 --> 00:00:33,262
file uploads, downloads, pre-signed URLs,

13
00:00:33,855 --> 00:00:35,731
and we'll explore all these things in a

14
00:00:35,779 --> 00:00:37,254
little demo application now.

15
00:00:38,023 --> 00:00:39,786
So again, I'm in a brand new demo

16
00:00:39,931 --> 00:00:42,527
application. You find it attached. And this

17
00:00:42,592 --> 00:00:44,804
demo application is all about a little

18
00:00:45,237 --> 00:00:49,356
backup CLI tool. So a little CLI tool we can

19
00:00:49,469 --> 00:00:52,739
run with some subcommands to back up an

20
00:00:52,851 --> 00:00:55,816
individual file, an entire folder, view the

21
00:00:55,816 --> 00:00:58,029
files we backed up to the cloud, and of

22
00:00:58,045 --> 00:01:00,642
course also restore them. Now, one word of

23
00:01:00,642 --> 00:01:02,790
caution right away. This is for demo

24
00:01:02,790 --> 00:01:05,163
purposes in this course. This is not a fully

25
00:01:05,195 --> 00:01:07,634
fledged backup tool. It will definitely miss

26
00:01:07,827 --> 00:01:10,025
some features you may want from your backup

27
00:01:10,073 --> 00:01:12,143
tool, though you could of course build up on

28
00:01:12,239 --> 00:01:14,710
it and evolve it into something more useful,

29
00:01:15,063 --> 00:01:17,357
but it gets its core job done.

30
00:01:18,111 --> 00:01:21,144
And to show you how it works, I'll first set

31
00:01:21,144 --> 00:01:23,871
up a backup bucket in the cloud because

32
00:01:23,871 --> 00:01:26,182
again, this interacts with the cloud with an

33
00:01:26,294 --> 00:01:28,925
S3-compatible store. That's the entire idea.

34
00:01:29,487 --> 00:01:31,797
And I'll use Cloudflare for that simply

35
00:01:31,813 --> 00:01:34,314
because that's super cheap. Indeed, you can

36
00:01:34,314 --> 00:01:36,493
get started for free if you're not uploading

37
00:01:36,542 --> 00:01:38,625
a lot. You need a credit card though,

38
00:01:39,266 --> 00:01:41,397
and it's easy to set up. So I'm in my

39
00:01:41,525 --> 00:01:43,945
Cloudflare account here, and there under

40
00:01:43,945 --> 00:01:46,060
Storage and Databases, you have the R2

41
00:01:46,236 --> 00:01:47,006
Object Storage.

42
00:01:47,855 --> 00:01:49,986
And under Overview, you can create a new

43
00:01:50,162 --> 00:01:50,435
bucket.

44
00:01:50,996 --> 00:01:52,791
So that's what I'll do, create a new bucket

45
00:01:53,223 --> 00:01:56,027
and name it backup-course-demo. The name is

46
00:01:56,027 --> 00:01:58,431
entirely up to you. Location, automatic.

47
00:01:58,752 --> 00:02:01,135
Default storage class is standard here.

48
00:02:02,761 --> 00:02:04,853
And of course, the setup steps will differ

49
00:02:04,934 --> 00:02:07,526
depending on which provider you use, but in

50
00:02:07,526 --> 00:02:09,808
the end, you just need to set up a bucket

51
00:02:09,938 --> 00:02:12,635
and then get some credentials for the app to

52
00:02:12,635 --> 00:02:14,715
connect to. But you'll see that in detail in

53
00:02:14,812 --> 00:02:15,446
just a second.

54
00:02:16,200 --> 00:02:19,366
So here the bucket was created, and now, as

